::GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Two figures have corresponding sides 3 and 6. What is the scale factor from the first figure to the second? {
=A. 2#The scale factor is 6 / 3 = 2.
~B. 3#This uses the first side length instead of the ratio.
~C. 1/2#This is the scale factor from the second figure to the first.
~D. 9#This adds the side lengths instead of dividing.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence supports figure similarity? {
~A. The figures are drawn close together.#Location does not prove similarity.
=B. Corresponding angles are congruent and corresponding sides are proportional.#This is the definition-level evidence for similarity.
~C. The figures have the same color.#Color is not a geometric property.
~D. Only one pair of sides is proportional.#One pair of sides is not enough to establish all side proportionality.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Side ratios are 2/4, 3/6, and 5/10. What do these ratios show? {
~A. Only the first sides match.#All three ratios matter, not only the first.
~B. The figures are congruent only.#Congruence would require scale factor 1.
=C. The side lengths are proportional.#Each ratio simplifies to 1/2, so the sides are proportional.
~D. The ratios are unequal.#The ratios are equal after simplifying.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is enlarged with scale factor 3. What happens to corresponding side lengths? {
~A. They are divided by 3.#Dividing by 3 would describe the reverse comparison.
~B. They stay the same.#Side lengths stay the same only for rigid motions.
~C. They become 3 units total.#Scale factor is a multiplier, not a final total length.
=D. They are multiplied by 3.#A scale factor of 3 multiplies each corresponding length by 3.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement avoids relying only on appearance? {
=A. The figures are similar because all corresponding side ratios equal 4/3.#Equal corresponding side ratios are mathematical evidence for similarity.
~B. The figures are similar because they look alike.#Looking alike is not enough evidence.
~C. The figures are similar because both are blue.#Color is not geometric evidence.
~D. The figures are similar because one is nearby.#Location is not similarity evidence.
}

